Vendored intermediate certificate -- Karnataka WCD portal TLS chain.

Source : http://secure.globalsign.com/cacert/gsgccr46ovtlsca2025.crt
         (the AIA "CA Issuers" URI the portal's own leaf certificate points at,
          fetched as DER and re-encoded to PEM)
Subject: C=BE, O=GlobalSign nv-sa, CN=GlobalSign GCC R46 OV TLS CA 2025
Issuer : C=BE, O=GlobalSign nv-sa, CN=GlobalSign Root R46
Validity: 2025-09-17 .. 2029-06-23
SHA-256 fingerprint:
  D1:60:E2:DE:4E:56:CB:10:B6:6C:1C:B0:AD:CB:79:CF:
  93:C7:8D:CD:9D:B3:0C:20:18:22:02:62:C0:40:63:F9

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
--------------------
wcd.karnataka.gov.in renews its leaf certificate onto GlobalSign's newer R46
intermediate but still serves the *previous* intermediate (GlobalSign RSA OV
SSL CA 2018) in its TLS handshake.  The chain it sends therefore does not
verify:

  $ openssl s_client -connect wcd.karnataka.gov.in:443
    Verify return code: 21 (unable to verify the first certificate)

Every ordinary client fails -- python-requests raises CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED
and `curl` exits 60.  Browsers only appear to work because they chase the AIA
extension and cache the missing intermediate themselves.

This file lets the crawler do the same thing without weakening anything: the
certificate is appended to the standard trust store, so the chain is still
cryptographically verified up to GlobalSign Root R46, which certifi already
trusts.  Verification is NOT disabled -- see `verify_for()` in pipeline/crawler.py.

WHEN TO DELETE
--------------
- when the portal starts serving a correct chain (then this entry, and
  `extra_ca` in pipeline/states.py, can both go), or
- when it expires on 2029-06-23, or
- when the issuer renews and the fingerprint above no longer matches.

This is a public CA certificate: it is not a secret and is safe to commit.

